export const REGION_HELP=`<p>Start with a preset and a seed, then change the layout before building.</p><h3>Layout and areas</h3><p>Drag locations to snap or swap them. Select an area to rename it, choose its habitat or change its connections. Custom settings let each settlement choose its own houses, designs, services and interiors. Press <b>Update location</b> to keep those changes.</p><p><b>Start the region here</b> sets the origin for progression. It changes the actual game's starting position only when the separate new-game option is enabled. Updating the overall plan or seed replaces the layout and area overrides.</p><h3>Terrain</h3><p>The climate preset uses temperature, rainfall and elevation. Other presets focus on a particular biome. Terrain switches control water, whole trees and rocks, one-way ledges, wild-ground patches and side paths. Cliff faces can be edited afterwards with the outdoor cliff brush.</p><h3>Progression and gameplay</h3><p>Expand <b>Progression & gameplay</b> for level ranges, encounters, people, landmarks, weather, music, saved milestones, supplies and trainers. Areas can override their levels and species pools. Game data → Wild Pokémon edits generated encounters by name after opening a supported source.</p><p>Choose trainer slots deliberately. Each selected slot is used once. Scaling or generating a team changes that trainer slot in the source game too. Saved events, native encounter tables and the new-game start use compatible source adapters; unsupported native features stop export with a specific message.</p><h3>Review and add</h3><p>Building is staged. Review the result, then add it as one undoable edit. Capacity fitting reduces houses and lists the changes; it does not silently remove selected facilities or floors.</p><p>Compile and inspect the exported artwork, then playtest. Large regions can use static artwork and packed generated tilesets. Connecting to the original world through a door uses less shared graphics space than a scrolling edge.</p>`;
